## 7. Timing Attack (Username Enumeration)

### Attack Example
```bash
# Attacker measures response time to determine if user exists
time curl -X POST http://localhost:3000/login \
  -d '{"username":"admin","password":"wrong"}'

time curl -X POST http://localhost:3000/login \
  -d '{"username":"nonexistent","password":"wrong"}'
```

### Protection (Recommended)
```javascript
const crypto = require('crypto');

app.post('/login', async (req, res) => {
  const { username, password } = req.body;

  // Always hash password, even if user doesn't exist
  const dummyHash = crypto.createHash('sha256').update('dummy').digest('hex');

  const user = await User.findOne({ username });
  const hash = user ? user.passwordHash : dummyHash;

  // Use constant-time comparison
  const inputHash = crypto.createHash('sha256').update(password).digest('hex');
  const isValid = crypto.timingSafeEqual(
    Buffer.from(hash),
    Buffer.from(inputHash)
  );

  // Same response regardless of whether user exists
  if (!user || !isValid) {
    return res.status(401).json({
      error: 'Unauthorized',
      message: 'Invalid credentials'
    });
  }

  // Login success...
});
```

